   "rect": [1030, 505, 1178, 620]},

  {"name": "Seafood counter customer aisle (audit 2026-07-27) — the ~26 pt (2.8 m) diagonal aisle a shopper stands in to be served at the seafood counter and to shop the two angled seafood cases (drawn polys (1073.9,431)-(1094.8,437.1) and (1094.2,451.4)-(1115.1,457.4)). It is bounded north-east by the counter's own outer wall (x=1102 down to y=418, then a 45-degree face to (1133,441)) and south-west by those two cases. The auto:SEAFOOD disk (pt (1127,399), r=130) uses the wider SEAFOOD_BRIDGE of 20 pt, which closed the 15-20 pt mouths at both ends of the aisle and around the cases, culling it as three pockets (165 cells at (1103,435), 73 at (1106,469), 25 at (1067,422)). The shipped grid therefore had NO floor in front of the counter's service face and none on the display side of either case — 12 pt to the nearest standable cell mid-aisle — which is also why the SEAFOOD anchor far-snapped 13 cells (2.8 m). The exclusion's own text already asserts this aisle 'stays walkable'; this rect makes that true. The staff prep block behind the counter is the exclusion poly and stays sealed (free_raw is already false there, so an inclusion cannot reach it), and the 8 pt interior band between the counter's two drawn lines stays walled off by that outer line — walk_truth pins it at (1119,433). The shape is a poly, not a rect, because its north-east edge has to track that outer counter line (x = 1100 + (y-418) for y in 418..444): a rect square enough to hold the whole aisle also swallows the 12 pt counter band above y=430, which came back as a 34-cell island and split the uncut grid into two components.",
